# What may enter the build context — an ALLOWLIST, not a list of exclusions.
#
# Written this way deliberately. A denylist ("ignore .env, ignore venv/") only
# protects against the files somebody thought of on the day they wrote it; the
# next secret added to this repo is included by default and nobody notices.
# Starting from `*` inverts that: a new file is OUT until someone deliberately
# lets it in, and letting it in is a line in a diff a reviewer can see.
#
# This matters more than usual here because a Docker layer is permanent. A file
# copied in one layer and deleted in a later one is STILL IN THE IMAGE — the
# delete only hides it from the final filesystem view. Anyone who pulls the
# image can read it back out of the earlier layer. So `.env` must never be
# copied in the first place; removing it afterwards would not help.
*

# ── what the running application actually needs ───────────────────────────
!app/
!alembic/
!alembic.ini
!requirements.txt

# ── re-excluded, having been swept in by the directory rules above ────────
# Last match wins, so these come after the re-inclusions.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/

# ── never, under any circumstances ────────────────────────────────────────
# Already covered by `*`, repeated so that the intent is explicit and so that
# a future `!` line cannot quietly undo it without a reviewer noticing.
.env
.env.*
.env.example
venv/
.git/
.github/
qa/
scripts/
*.md
*.log
*.pdf
*.json
